00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:07.430 align:start position:0%
european union finance and economy  ministers will strive this week to forge  a common approach on new rules to  preempt bank crises  the aim is to help a struggling credit  institution or investment firm recover  from any shock in an orderly manner  preserving its operations and minimizing  the possibility of taxpayer losses  at the next council in luxembourg the  irish presidency is determined to seal  agreement on rules that would require  banks and investment firms to draw up  recovery plans and lay out the measures  they would take to restore their  financial health if things turn sour  the ministers will debate the use and  size of a resolution fund for direct  solvency support the measures would  establish a bail-in order for investors  to help bail out an institution in  strife without relying on small savings  holders once sealed this common council  approach would form the starting point  for negotiations on bank recovery and  resolution with the european parliament  also topping the agenda will be the  council's expected approval of  recommendations on the economic and  fiscal policies of 23 eu member states  the recommendations made by the european  commission are part of the european  semester program they're aimed at  strengthening national budgets in a  coordinated way and thereby bolstering  the euro single currency
 

